  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 157 PM CDT Fri Oct 16 2026\n\n18z/1pm surface analysis shows a 998mb low over southern Quebec\nwith a cold front extending southward to the Gulf Coast. \nMeanwhile a sprawling 1046 high is building into the Plains. The\npressure gradient between the departing front and the\napproaching high has resulted in strong/gusty northwesterly\nwinds across central Illinois today. The latest regional\nobservations continue to indicate gusts of 25-30mph and these\nwill continue for the next few hours before gradually beginning\nto diminish toward sunset. Ample cloud cover blankets the region\nas a trough axis drops southward out of Wisconsin/Michigan. Once\nthe trough sinks south of central Illinois, synoptic subsidence\non its back side will allow skies to at least partially clear \nby mid to late evening...followed by clear skies across nearly\nthe entire KILX CWA overnight. The exception will be along and\neast of a Danville to Casey line where a persistent lake-effect\nband will result in continued partly to mostly cloudy conditions\nthrough dawn Saturday. While skies will become clear elsewhere,\nwinds will remain in the 10-15mph range through the evening. As\nthe pressure gradient continues to loosen, winds will \neventually drop below 10mph and may become light/variable across\nthe Illinois River Valley toward daybreak. With lows dropping \ninto the lower to middle 30s and overnight winds diminishing, \nhave opted to continue the Frost Advisory for locations \nalong/west of a Lacon...to Lincoln...to Taylorville line.\n\nHigh pressure will build overhead on Saturday, resulting in\nsunny skies and light winds with chilly highs in the upper 50s\nto around 60 degrees. While winds will be calm Saturday evening,\na light S/SE return flow will develop across the western half of\nthe CWA overnight as the high slowly shifts eastward into the\nOhio River Valley. With clear skies and very light winds, it\nappears Saturday night will feature a better shot at widespread\nfrost across all of central and southeast Illinois. A Frost\nAdvisory will likely be needed.\n\nAfter that, the rest of the 7-day forecast looks quiet as upper\nridging builds over the Midwest. While an occasional weak\nshort-wave trough may temporarily impede the process and bring a\nfew clouds and perhaps even a light shower, the overall pattern\nis decidedly warmer and drier. Highs will start out near \nseasonal normals in the middle to upper 60s on Sunday...then \nwill approach 80 degrees by next Wednesday-Friday.",
